{"schemaVersion":1,"seq":24,"prev":"1ed38fa83014c7db17e34933f532c7a7e92ab72b7b8a66e19b11cad3b7cc2594","at":"2026-09-27T04:06:54.074549Z","type":"node_output","state":"recheck","iter":0,"node":"recheck","data":{"output":{"findings":[{"tag":"advisory","file":"internal/repo/worktree_test.go","start_line":69,"end_line":75,"issue":"TestRunStoreReadersAreDeclared checks whole files and matches only the exact literal `\".metareview\", \"runs\"`. cmd/metareview/main.go already contains `RunStoreRoot(`, so a second current-worktree reader added to main.go passes, and a path spelled differently (\".metareview/runs\", a constant) is never inspected. The worktree.go doc comment (lines 18-20) claims the test enforces the rule for any reader, which is stronger than what it checks.","consequence":"The #169 regression guard can miss a new reader in main.go, the most likely place for one, and the comment overstates how much protection the test gives.","confidence":60,"severity":"P3"},{"tag":"advisory","file":"internal/repo/worktree.go","start_line":33,"end_line":58,"issue":"RunStoreRoot's comment says it resolves the main worktree 'exactly as the FSM resolves it', but runStoreGit runs `git worktree list --porcelain` with the caller's full environment, while the FSM writer (rootOf via gate.RealExec) strips GIT_* variables (scrubEnv) and sets GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1 and LC_ALL=C. The two share the parser but not the git invocation.","consequence":"If record-lenses runs with GIT_DIR, GIT_COMMON_DIR or GIT_WORK_TREE exported (from a git hook or a wrapper), it can resolve a different main worktree than the FSM wrote to, and the #169 'no such FSM run' failure comes back under those conditions.","confidence":40,"severity":"P3"}]}}}
